Linux
---------

Linux is an operating system commonly used to run enterprise applications and servers.

For example, our Java application might run on a Linux server with Apache Tomcat/JBoss installed.

As an engineer, we use Linux commands to:

- Navigate directories.
- Check application logs.
- Search for errors.
- Check whether processes are running.
- Check disk and memory usage.
- Investigate application failures.

A. Basic Linux commands
-------------------------
pwd - Show current directory > pwd
ls  -	List files > ls -l
cd  - Change directory > cd /opt/jboss
mkdir - 	Create directory > mkdir logs
touch -	Create empty file	touch > test.txt
cp - Copy files > cp a.txt b.txt
mv - Move/rename files > mv old.txt new.txt
rm - Delete files > rm test.txt
cat - Display file content > cat app.log
less - Read large files	> less app.log
clear - Clear > terminal	clear

B. Most important: Log troubleshooting commands
-------------------------------------------------

1. tail

> Displays the last lines of a file.
> tail -100 server.log
Displays the last 100 lines.

2. tail -f

> Continuously monitors a log file.
> tail -f server.log

Imagine a user reports that an API is failing.
We can execute this command and observe new log entries as requests are processed.

3. grep

> Searches for a particular word or pattern.
> grep "ERROR" server.log
Search for errors.

> grep -i "exception" server.log
Search for exceptions, ignoring case.

> grep -i "NullPointerException" server.log
Find NullPointerException occurrences.

> grep -n "ERROR" server.log
Display the line numbers with ERROR

> grep -in "exception" server.log
Searches for exception by ignoring the case and alos displays line numbers

4. Find

> Search for files.
> find /opt -name "server.log"
Finds server.log under /opt.

5. Head

> Displays the first lines.
> head -50 server.log

6. wc

> Counts lines, words and characters.
> wc -l server.log
Counts the number of lines.

